(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-ent-analgesics-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Analgesics & Anti-inflammatories Quiz 2 — Pharmacology I Exam 2","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which organ systems carry ibuprofen's main adverse effects?","choices":["The liver and the bone marrow","The lungs and the thyroid","The skin and the joints","The gastrointestinal tract and the kidney"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ulceration and bleeding at one end, falling clearance and oedema at the other -- and both come from the same prostaglandin blockade. Fluid retention and oedema come with the renal effects. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which finding marks the most advanced stage of Reye's syndrome?","choices":["Cerebral oedema with coma","Fatty liver with encephalitis","Seizures and multiple organ failure","Vomiting with lethargy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The staging runs from a nonspecific febrile illness to multi-organ failure, which is why the early stage matters so much. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"How does aspirin act on cyclooxygenase, and what follows from it?","choices":["Irreversibly and non-competitively, so the platelet never recovers","Reversibly, so the effect ends as the drug is cleared","Selectively at cyclooxygenase-2 only","Indirectly, by reducing prostaglandin release from storage"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A platelet has no nucleus and cannot make fresh enzyme, so one exposure disables it for its lifetime. Everything distinctive about aspirin as an antiplatelet follows from that. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A patient on lithium is started on ibuprofen. What is the concern?","choices":["Ibuprofen reduces lithium excretion, so levels rise","Ibuprofen blocks lithium absorption, so levels fall","The two combine to cause serotonin syndrome","Lithium increases the risk of gastric ulceration from ibuprofen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Lithium has little margin between useful and toxic, so anything that slows its removal matters quickly. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What underlying cellular abnormality is described in Reye's syndrome?","choices":["Mitochondrial dysfunction in brain","Autoimmune destruction of hepatocytes","Direct viral invasion of the brain","Accumulation of unconjugated bilirubin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. One failing organelle across several tissues explains why an illness of the liver and brain also involves muscle. Liver and muscle. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which situations are listed as contraindications to aspirin?","choices":["Asthma, renal impairment and peptic ulcer alone","Bleeding disorders, pregnancy","Any patient over sixty-five","Concurrent acetaminophen use"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Three unrelated mechanisms -- bleeding risk, pregnancy, and Reye's -- which is why the list has to be remembered rather than derived. Children with fever from viral illness. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Is aspirin selective for one cyclooxygenase isoform?","choices":["Yes, it is COX-2 selective","Yes, it is COX-1 selective","It does not act on cyclooxygenase at all","No, it is non-selective for COX-1 and COX-2"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hitting COX-1 as well is where the gastric and bleeding problems come from. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What three actions does ibuprofen have?","choices":["Analgesic and antipyretic only","Anti-inflammatory, analgesic and antipyretic","Anti-inflammatory and antiplatelet only","Antipyretic only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. All three at ordinary use, unlike aspirin where they separate out by exposure. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What happens when ibuprofen is combined with an anticoagulant?","choices":["The anticoagulant is inactivated","Absorption of both drugs is reduced","Clotting time shortens","Prothrombin time is prolonged"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ibuprofen damages the mucosa and the anticoagulant stops the bleeding from being controlled, which is why the combination is worse than either alone. With a risk of serious gastrointestinal bleeding. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A child in the earliest stage of Reye's syndrome would show which of these?","choices":["A rash on the hands and feet","Coma with fixed, dilated pupils","Seizures and multiple organ failure","Encephalitis with hyperventilation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Stage one looks nonspecific, which is exactly the problem -- it is the stage at which the illness is most treatable and least recognisable. Vomiting, high fever and lethargy. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A patient on a diuretic and an ACE inhibitor starts taking ibuprofen regularly. What should be watched?","choices":["Liver enzymes and bilirubin","Thyroid function","Renal function","Serum potassium only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Two interactions at once: the kidney is at risk with the diuretic, and the ACE inhibitor works less well. The blood pressure response to the ACE inhibitor. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"How is acetaminophen tolerated when taken as intended?","choices":["Poorly, with frequent gastric irritation","With common renal impairment","With frequent sedation","Very well, with no notable side effects"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Its safety within the intended range is exactly why exceeding that range is so easy to do. That holds at therapeutic exposure, which is what makes exceeding it so easy. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Who is affected by Reye's syndrome, and how dangerous is it?","choices":["Adults over sixty-five, with mortality around a tenth","Children, with mortality around half","Children, but it is almost never fatal","Any age, with mortality under one per cent"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The severity is what justifies a blanket restriction on a cheap and otherwise useful drug. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"How is acetaminophen classified, and what does that exclude?","choices":["Analgesic, antipyretic and anti-inflammatory","Analgesic and antipyretic","Anti-inflammatory and antiplatelet","Antipyretic only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. That missing third action is the practical difference from the non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, and it decides when it is not the right choice. But not anti-inflammatory. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Ibuprofen is avoided below a certain age. Which group?","choices":["All children under twelve","All children under fifteen, because of Reye's syndrome","Young infants","Adolescents only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. There is a lower age limit below which it is not used, which distinguishes it from acetaminophen. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A patient is started on aspirin for fever. What should they be told about other medicines?","choices":["Do not take other anti-inflammatory drugs alongside it","Take an antacid alongside every dose","Avoid acetaminophen entirely while taking it","Take it only on a completely empty stomach"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. They share a mechanism, so combining them adds the harms without adding the benefit. That means the non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs specifically. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"How is aspirin handled by the body?","choices":["Absorbed orally and excreted unchanged in bile","Absorbed through the skin and metabolised in plasma","Absorbed orally","Absorbed orally and eliminated entirely by the lungs"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Liver then kidney, which is why disease in either changes what a given exposure does. Conjugated in the liver, excreted by the kidney. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 15"}]};
